## 1. INTRODUCYION
With the intensification of energy crisis and greenhouse effect, the development and utilization of renewable and clean energy sources has aroused great interest among researchers. Hydrogen gas $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\right)$ is considered as an ideal alternative energy to fossil fuels.[1, 2] Currently, electrocatalytic water splitting is an effective and clean method for large-scale $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production. The hydrogen evolution reaction at the cathodic (HER: $2\mathrm{H}^{+}+2\mathrm{e}^{-}\rightarrow\mathrm{H}_{2}$ ) and the oxygen evolution reaction at the anodic (OER: $4\mathrm{OH}^{-}\to\mathrm{O}_{2}+4\mathrm{e}^{-}+2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ are the two half-reactions of electrocatalytic water splitting.[3-6] However, the OER is thermodynamically unfavourable and kinetically sluggish, which is considered to be the rate-limiting step, which involves complex multi-proton and multi-electron transfer processes.[7-9] Using highly efficiency electrocatalysts is an effective way to solve this problem. Noble-metal-based materials $\mathrm{TrO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ ) are currently regarded as the most effective catalysts for OER.[10, 11] Unfortunately, suffering from high price and scarcity, their wide applications are seriously hampered. To this end, many non-noble metal catalysts have been prepared by transition metal elements, including transition metal oxides (TMO),[12] transition metal sulfides (TMS),[13] transition metal phosphides (TMP)[14] and many other transition metal compounds. Although some achievements have been made, the development of efficient, stable and low-cost OER electrocatalysts still faces great challenges.
Among them, great efforts have been made to catalysts based on transition metal phosphides, such as CoP, FeP, ${\bf N i}_{2}{\bf P}$ , and MoP. Phosphides are composed of metallic elements and phosphorus with superior electrical conductivity and excellent stability, both of which are conducive for their catalytic performance.[15-16] For example, porous CoP concave polyhedron were reported by Dong’s group which require an overpotential of $133~\mathrm{mV}$ at current density of $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ toward HER in acid media.[17] Li’s group reported carbon-coated hollow mesoporous FeP microcubes which show a low overpotential of $115~\mathrm{mV}$ at a current density of $10\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ for the HER in 0.5 M ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ electrolyte.[18] Wang’s group reported sandwich-like CoP/C nanocomposites which show a low overpotential of only $330~\mathrm{mV}$ at a current density of $10\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ for the OER in 1 M KOH electrolyte.[19] However, due to the single composition, their catalytic performances are still far lower than that of noble-metal-based catalysts. In order to further improve the electrochemical activity, bimetallic phosphides were studied to achieve highly catalytic activity. Nanoporous bimetallic $(\mathrm{Co}_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{x}})_{2}\mathrm{P}$ phosphides synthesized by Chen’s group can serve as catalyst towards full water splitting to show an overpotential of $270\mathrm{mV}$ for OER and $64\mathrm{mV}$ for HER at $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ .[20] Sea urchin-like cobalt-iron phosphides were reported to show a overpotential of $370~\mathrm{mV}$ for OER at $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ .[21] Experimental results and theoretical calculation have indicated that tailoring the electronic structure by elemental doping can enhance their electrochemical performance due to a synergistic effect.[22-25] The amount of doped metal plays an important role in the modification of electronic structure. It is important to investigate the different doping amount for improving the catalytic activity of the catalysts. Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), a class of crystalline hybrid materials consisting of inorganic and organic components and possessing periodic structural units with coordination bonds, which have been widely regarded as an ideal precursor to produce advanced electrochemical energy storage and conversion materials.[26-30] Generally speaking, the preparation of advanced electrode materials using MOF as a precursor has the following advantages. First, most MOFs can maintain their original morphology after pyrolysis at low temperatures. Different morphologies of microstructures and nanostructures can be easily synthesized by using MOF as template. Second, the metal composition and carbon/nitrogen are uniformly distributed in the regular structure of MOF, which can not be achieved by simply mixing carbon/nitrogen with materials. This uniform composition improves the electrochemical active surface area and effective electron transport rate in the electrochemical process. Third, heteroatoms in MOF can also improve electrochemical performance by providing more active sites and higher conductivity. Although a large number of MOFs have been reported, covering a variety of metal species and organic ligands, the precise synthesis of MOFs with different metal ratios remains a challenge. In this study, $\mathrm{Co_{x}F e_{1}}$ $\mathbf{\nabla}_{\mathbf{x}}\mathbf{P}$ catalysts were successfully synthesized by low-temperature phosphorization of corresponding MOF precursors. The synergistic effect between Co and Fe elements results in enhancing OER activity. Furthermore, the presence of phosphorus in the compounds lead to metalloid characteristic, which is greatly improved the electrical conductivity. With the precise control of the chemical composition, a series of $\mathrm{Co_{x}F e_{1-x}P}$ $\scriptstyle\sum=0.33$ , 0.50, 0.66, 0.75 and 0.80) have been synthesized. Among them, the as-prepared $\mathrm{Co}_{0.66}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.33}\mathrm{P}$ catalyst shows a low overpotential of only $294\mathrm{mV}$ at a current density of $10\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , which is not only better than many reported bimetal phosphides Table S1, but also comparable to noble-metal-based OER catalysts.
## 2. EXPERIMENTAL
## 2.1 Materials
Cobalt(II)perchlorate hydrate, iron(II)perchlorate hydrate, formic acid and $[\mathrm{C}(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{3}]_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3}$ were purchased from commercial sources. All materials were used as purchased without further purification.
## 2.2 Synthesis of cube-like $\mathrm{\small{[C(NH_{2})_{3}][C o(H C O O)_{3}]}}$ precursor
Briefly, 10 mmol of formic acid and $4.2\ \mathrm{mmol}$ of $[\mathrm{C}(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{3}]_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3}$ were dissolved in $25~\mathrm{mL}$ ethanol-water (4:1 v/v) solution. After that, an ethanol-water $(4{:}1\ \mathrm{v/v})$ solution ( $25~\mathrm{mL}$ ) contain 1 mmol cobalt(II)perchlorate hydrate was added to the solution. The mixed solution was stirred for 1 h and aged for $12\mathrm{{h}}$ at room temperature before centrifuged, washed with ethanol several times and dried at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight.
## 2.3 Synthesis of cube-like $\mathrm{\sfIC(NH_{2})_{3}}][\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{HCOO_{3}}]$ precursor
The synthesis of $[\mathrm{C(NH_{2})_{3}}][\mathrm{Fe(HCOO)_{3}}]$ is similar to above method, except for using iron(II) perchlorate hydrate as the metal source.
2.4 Synthesis of cube-like $[\mathbf{C}(\mathbf{NH}_{2})_{3}][\mathbf{Co}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathbf{Fe}_{1-\mathrm{x}}(\mathbf{HCOO})_{3}]$ precursor
The synthesis of $[\mathrm{C(NH_{2})_{3}}][\mathrm{Co_{x}F e_{1-x}(H C O O)_{3}}]$ is similar to above method, except for using
cobalt(II)perchlorate hydrate and iron(II) perchlorate hydrate with a mole ratio of x:1-x (0.33:0.66, 0.50:0.50, 0.66:0.33, 0.75:0.25, and 0.8:0.2).
## 2.5 Synthesis of cube-like CoP, FeP, or $\mathbf{Co_{x}F e_{1-x}P}$
In brief, $20~\mathrm{\mg}$ of the as-prepared $\mathrm{\small{[C(NH_{2})_{3}][C o(H C O O)_{3}]}}$ , [C(NH2)3][Fe(HCOO)3] or $[\mathrm{C(NH_{2})_{3}}][\mathrm{Co_{x}F e_{1-x}(H C O O)_{3}}]$ precursors were placed next to $200~\mathrm{\mg}$ of ${\mathrm{NaH}}_{2}{\mathrm{PO}}_{2}$ at the downstream side of the furnace. Then the furnace was heated to $350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2\textrm{h}$ under a $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ atmosphere with a heating rate of $1\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\ \mathrm{min^{-1}}$ . Finally, CoP, FeP, or $\mathrm{Co}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Fe}_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{P}$ were harvested respectively when it cooled to room temperature.
## 2.6 Electrochemical Measurements
The performances of as-prepared samples were studied on a WaveDriver 20 potentiostat/galvanostat system (PINE Research Instrumentation) at room temperature in a three-electrode system. Here, a modified glassy carbon rotation disk electrode (GC, RDE, 5 mm diameter, $0.196\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ ), a $\mathrm{\sfAg/AgCl}$ and a graphite rod were used as working electrode, counter electrode and reference electrode. For the preparation of working electrode, $2.5~\mathrm{mg}$ of the as-prepared samples with $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ Nafion were dispersed in $490\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of 1:1 (v/v) ethanol and distilled water mixed solution under ultrasonicated for $30\mathrm{min}$ . Then $10\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of the ink was dropped onto the surface of the GC electrode and dried naturally. The mass loading of the catalysts are approximately $0.25\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . The overpotential was calculated by the equation: η=ERHE-1.23 V. Potentials repored in this work were referenced to a reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) using the equation $\mathrm{E}_{R H E}{=}\mathrm{E}_{A g/A g C l}{+}0.059p H.$ Before the electrochemical tests, the working electrode was first tested by cyclic voltammetry (CV) through the potential window of 0.2 to $0.8\mathrm{V}$ vs. $\mathrm{\sfAg/AgCl}$ at a scan rate of $100\mathrm{mV\s^{-1}}$ until the CV curves were steady. Subsequently, the polarization curves were obtained from 0.2 to $0.8\mathrm{~V~}$ vs. $\mathrm{\sfAg/AgCl}$ at a rotation speed of $1600\mathrm{rpm}$ with a scan rate of ${5\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{s}^{-1}}$ . For the stability tests, the normalized amperometric i-t curves were tested at $1.524\mathrm{V}$ vs. RHE. The EIS tests were carried out at 1.51 V vs. RHE from $10^{5}$ to $0.01\mathrm{Hz}$ with $5\mathrm{mV}$ . For comparison, ${\mathrm{RuO}}_{2}$ catalyst was prepared. The preparation method of $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ catalyst is the same as that of $\mathrm{Co}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Fe}_{1\mathrm{-x}}\mathrm{P}$ catalyst. All data were used without iR compensation.
## 3. RESULT AND DISSCUSSION
The $\mathrm{Co_{x}F e_{1-x}P}$ catalysts were synthesized by a two-step method as shown in Scheme 1. First, five kinds of MOF precursors with different ratio of Co and Fe atoms was successfully synthesized by room temperature liquid method, in which organic monoammonium cations acted as template and formic acid served as organic ligand to connect Co and Fe atoms. They were defined as $\mathrm{\small{[C(NH_{2})_{3}][C o_{0.33}F e_{0.66}(H C O O)_{3}],}}$ $\mathrm{[C(NH_{2})_{3}][C o_{0.50}F e_{0.50}(H C O O)_{3}]},$ $\mathrm{\small{[C(NH_{2})_{3}][C o_{0.66}F e_{0.33}(H C O O)_{3}],}}$ [C(NH2)3][Co0.75Fe0.25(HCOO)3] and $\mathrm{[C(NH_{2})_{3}][C o_{0.80}F e_{0.20}(H C O O)_{3}]}$ when the mole ratio of cobalt(II) perchlorate hydrate and iron(II) perchlorate hydrate were 1:2, 1:1, 2:1, 3:1 and 4:1, respectively. Then $\mathrm{Co}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Fe}_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{P}$ catalysts were obtained though a solid-state reaction between $\mathrm{PH}_{3}$ and corresponding MOFs precursors. With the increase of temperature, $\mathrm{NaH}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{2}$ located upstream of tubular furnace decomposes into phosphine gas with strong reducibility and reacts with precursors located downstream to form $\mathrm{Co}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Fe}_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{P}$ .
The morphologies of these precursors were tested by scanning electron microscopy as shown in Fig. 1. These uniform precursors have a cube-like shape and the average diameters of the cube are approximately $5\mu\mathrm{m}$ . Additionally, pure Co and Fe precursors were also tested as shown in Fig. S1a, b. EDX elemental mapping (Fig. $\mathbf{1c}_{1}$ and Fig. S1e, f) shows that the presence of Co, Fe and P are homogeneously distributed on the $\mathrm{Co}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Fe}_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{P}$ cube. X-ray diffraction measurements were further applied to demonstrate the composition of these precursors. As shown in Fig. 2a and Fig. S2a, all precursors show typical characteristic diffraction peaks, which can be assigned well to simulated isomorphic crystal structure. The peaks positions are consistent with the pure Co or Fe precursors, indicating that the precursors have been successfully fabricated.
A one step low-temperature phosphidation strategy was further carried out to synthesized a series of $\mathrm{Co}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Fe}_{1\mathrm{-x}}\mathrm{P}$ compounds via phosphidation of corresponding MOF precursors, whereas for comparison, pure CoP and FeP were also synthesized via phosphidation of $\mathrm{\small{[C(NH_{2})_{3}][C o(H C O O)_{3}]}}$ and $\mathrm{\small{[C(NH_{2})_{3}][F e(H C O O)_{3}]}}$ . After phosphidation, some new strong diffraction peaks appeared, which located at $31.6^{\mathrm{o}}$ , 36.3o, $46.2^{\mathrm{{o}}}$ and $48.1^{\mathrm{o}}$ can be corresponded to (011), (111), (112), and (211) lattice planes of CoP (powder diffraction file no. 29-0497), as shown in Fig. 2b and Fig. S2b, c. No other diffraction peaks were observed, proving the high purity of the products. Moreover, the different intensities of characteristic peaks indicate that the cobalt-ironratio is very important for the formation of well-defined crystal phase.
Energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX) were used to investigate the elemental compositions of the $\mathrm{Co_{x}F e_{1-x}P}$ . P peaks were clearly observed (Fig. 3 and Fig. S3), indicating that the Co/Fe-MOF had been successfully phosphided. Co and Fe peaks were also clearly observed, and the weight ratio of Co to Fe was close to the theoretical cobalt-iron ratio.
SEM images (Fig. 4a, b, c, d and e) show that all the products inherited the cube morphology after phosphidation but their average diameters decreased. The average size of the synthesized $\mathrm{Co}_{0.66}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.33}\mathrm{P}$ catalyst is about $4~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ (Fig. S4). We can clearly see that the smooth surfaces of the precursors convert to rough and the compact morphology converts to porosity after phosphidation. Based on the TEM images (Fig. S5), we can see that $\mathrm{Co_{x}F e_{1-x}P}$ catalysts are composed of small particles, which connect with each other to form a porous morphology. In comparison, $\mathrm{Co}_{0.66}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.33}\mathrm{p}$ is more porous than other samples. Moreover, from the nitrogen adsorption-desorption isotherms curves (Fig. S6a), the BET method surface area is calculated to be $21.6\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for CoP, $25.0\:\mathrm{m}^{2}\:\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for FeP and $34.5~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{Co}_{0.66}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.33}\mathrm{P}$ . The pore size distribution histogram(Fig. S6b) confirm the mesoporous structure of $\mathrm{Co}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Fe}_{1\mathrm{-x}}\mathrm{P}$ and the mian pore diameter is $3.41{-}3.83\ \mathrm{nm}$ . This proves that the proportion of cobalt and iron has a great influence on the structure of the products, which results in better OER activity. HRTEM images (Fig. 4f) exhibit the fringe spacing of $0.19~\mathrm{nm}$ that can correspond to the (211) plane of the $\mathrm{Co_{x}P_{1-x}P}$ phase. It is consistent with the above XRD results that further demonstrate $\mathrm{Co}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Fe}_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{P}$ have been successfully synthesized.
For studying the chemical composition of the as-synthesized $\mathrm{Co}_{0.66}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.33}\mathrm{P}$ sample, XPS measurements were performed, for comparison CoP and FeP were also tested. From the survey spectrum (Fig. 5a), we can observe the existence of Co, Fe, P, and O elements (The presence of O element is due to exposure of samples to air.), matching well with the above EDX results. Focus on the Co 2p of CoP (Fig. 5b), two species of Co along with two satellite peaks can be observed. The peaks located at $793.6\mathrm{eV}$ and 778.7 eV, 798.1 eV and 780.2 eV, 802.9 eV and $785.7\mathrm{eV}$ are assigned to the Co $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ , $\mathrm{Co}2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ of $\mathrm{Co}$ species in Co-P, the Co $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ , Co $2{\tt p}_{3/2}$ of the oxidized cobalt species, two satellite peaks, respectively.[31, 32] Based on the Fe 2p of FeP (Fig. 5c), two main peaks located at 711.2 and $724.3\mathrm{eV}$ are corresponded to Fe $2{\tt p}_{3/2}$ and Fe $2{\mathsf{p}}_{1/2}$ , indicating that the presence of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ . The peak appears at 717.8 ev is assigned to a satellite peak.[33, 34] Concentrate on the $\mathrm{~P~}2\mathsf{p}$ of CoP and FeP (Fig. 5d), the spectrum can be divided into three peaks, which located at 133.8, 130.0 and $129.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ can be assigned to the bond energies of $\mathrm{P-O}$ , P $2{\tt p}_{3/2}$ and P $2{\mathsf{p}}_{1/2}$ in phosphides and phosphorus oxide, respectively.[35, 36] Importantly, compared with the binding energies of pure CoP and FeP, P $2{\tt p}_{3/2}$ and Fe $2{\tt p}_{3/2}$ binding energies of $\mathrm{Co}_{0.66}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.33}\mathrm{P}$ show negative shifts and $\mathrm{Co}~2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ shows positive shifts. These shifts indicate cationic state of Co atoms and the anionic state of the P and Fe atoms.[37-39] Compared with that only have one in pure CoP or FeP, the active site reaches two in $\mathrm{Co}_{0.66}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.33}\mathrm{P}$ because of the negative shifts. These negative shifts signify the increased electron occupation and result in stronger electron donating ability, which are good for OER. [40, 41] Overall, the incorporation of a binary transition metal could cause the redistribution of valence, which promotes the proton-discharging process in OER.
Further, we investigated the OER catalytic properties of as-prepared samples in a $\Nu_{2}$ -saturated 1 M KOH aqueous solution at room temperature. Fig. 6a shows polarization curves of the as-prepared samples and ${\mathrm{RuO}}_{2}$ at a scan rate of ${5\mathrm{mVs^{-1}}}$ , it can be seen that $\mathrm{Co}_{0.66}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.33}\mathrm{P}$ shows the lower onset potential and higher current density than other samples. From the polarization curves at lower overpotentials (Fig. 6b), it can be obviously seen that the $\mathrm{Co}_{0.66}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.33}\mathrm{P}$ can deliver a current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ at an overpotential of $294~\mathrm{mV}$ , which is lower than that of CoP at $314~\mathrm{mV}$ , $\mathrm{Co}_{0.33}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.66}\mathrm{P}$ at $408~\mathrm{{mV}}$ , $\mathrm{Co}_{0.50}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.50}\mathrm{P}$ at $425\mathrm{mV}$ , $\mathrm{Co}_{0.75}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.25}\mathrm{P}$ at $315\mathrm{mV}$ , $\mathrm{Co}_{0.80}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.20}\mathrm{P}$ at $328\mathrm{mV}$ and FeP at $388~\mathrm{mV}$ . These results further indicating that the Co element and Fe element in $\mathrm{Co}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Fe}_{1}$ - $\mathbf{\nabla}_{\mathbf{x}}\mathbf{P}$ have a synergistic effect. The presence of Co and Fe modified the electronic structure resulting in fast electron transfer, which lowers the kinetic energy for the OER reactions. [42, 43] The Tafel plots calculated from the polarization curves are shown in Fig. 6c. Compare with that of $103~\mathrm{{mV}}$ dec-1 for CoP, $90~\mathrm{mV}$ dec-1 for $\mathrm{Co}_{0.33}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.66}\mathrm{P}$ , $103~\mathrm{mV}$ dec-1 for $\mathrm{Co}_{0.50}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.50}\mathrm{P}$ , $107~\mathrm{mV}$ dec-1 for $\mathrm{Co}_{0.75}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.25}\mathrm{P}$ , 81 mV dec-1 Co0.80Fe0.20P, $78~\mathrm{mV}$ dec-1 for FeP and 90 mV dec-1 for $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ , the Tafel slope of $\mathrm{Co}_{0.66}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.33}\mathrm{p}$ reaches $69\mathrm{mV\dec^{-1}}$ , which profits from faster electron transport, indicating a more rapid OER rate on the $\mathrm{Co}_{0.66}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.33}\mathrm{P}$ catalyst. These Tafel slopes suggest that the OER proceeds through a Volmer-Heyrovsky mechanism, and the electrochemical desorption process is the rate-limiting step. As a critical parameter for OER, the stability of the $\mathrm{Co}_{0.66}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.33}\mathrm{P}$ catalyst was tested. The normalized amperometric i-t curves were tested at an overpotential of $294~\mathrm{mV}$ for $6\mathrm{~h~}$ and the results are show in Fig. 6d. It can be seen that no significant loss is observed during $6\mathrm{n}$ . Besides, we further tested the polarization curves after cycling from 0.2 to $0.8\mathrm{~V~}$ for 1000 cycles. Obviously, the line after 1000 cycles is matching well with the initial line. These results indicate that $\mathrm{Co}_{0.66}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.33}\mathrm{P}$ has an excellent durability and may practically applicable. In order to prove the stability and durability of the catalyst, SEM analysis was performed to test whether there are morphological changes after OER cycles. SEM images (Fig. S7) show that the cubic shape of the catalyst remained well after OER cycle, and some holes on the surface appeared, which may be related to the electrochemical reaction on the catalyst.
Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements were further carried out at 1.51 V vs.
RHE to provide an insight into the electrode reaction kinetics of the as-prepared products. The corresponding Nyquist plots are show in Fig. 7. A small semicircle suggests a low charge transfer resistance at the interface between electrode and electrolyte.[44] It can be obviously seen that $\mathrm{Co}_{0.66}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.33}\mathrm{P}$ shows the smallest semicircle, matching well with its best OER performances. As a key parameter, electrochemical surface area (ECSA) is closely related to the electrochemical performance, which is proportional to the electrochemical double-layer capacitance $\mathrm{(C_{dl}).}^{[42]}\mathrm{C_{dl}}$ are extracted by plotting the $\scriptstyle\Delta j=j_{a}-j_{c}$ at a given potential ( $1.2\mathrm{V}$ vs. RHE) against the CV scan rates (20- $100\ \mathrm{mV\s^{-1}}$ ). The $\mathrm{C_{dl}}$ values calculated from cyclic voltammograms are shown in Fig. S8. It can clearly see that the $\mathrm{C_{dl}}$ values of $\mathrm{Co}_{0.66}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.33}\mathrm{P}$ is $15.51\mathrm{mF}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , higher than that of $5.78~\mathrm{mF~cm^{-2}}$ for CoP and $4.39~\mathrm{mF~cm^{-2}}$ for FeP. Combined the high $\mathrm{C_{dl}}$ values and above the low charge transfer resistance, thus contributing to the excellent OER performance of the $\mathrm{Co}_{0.66}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.33}\mathrm{P}$ catalyst.
In order to further explore the OER mechanism, the XPS spectroscopy of the $\mathrm{Co}_{0.66}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.33}\mathrm{P}$ catalyst after cycling was performed, and the results were shown in Fig. 8. In the high resolution XPS spectra of Co 2p and Fe 2p (Fig. 8a, b), the metal phosphides peaks almost disappeared completely after OER. Meanwhile, some new peaks corresponding to Co-O, and Fe-O species can be observed. Based on the P 2p spectra after OER (Fig. 8c), the P 2p signal disappeared completely, suggesting that all the P species on the surface of the catalyst were transformed to $\mathrm{PO}_{4}{}^{3-}$ and dissolved into the electrolyte at the high electric potential conditions. According to the O 1s spectra (Fig. 8d), P-O species with higher intensity is observed on the fresh catalyst, followed by the post-OER samples. The dominant contribution at $529.6~\mathrm{eV}$ corresponding to the oxygen from metal oxides appears, along with the typical surface hydroxyl species at $531.0\mathrm{eV}$ . These results indicate that Co-Fe-P was transformed into Co-Fe oxide/hydroxide and acts as the active site for OER. Moreover, a small amount of Fe doping can adjust the surface electronic structure. However, due to the weaker OER activity of Fe than that of Co, excessive Fe doping can lead to the decrease of OER performance.
## 4. CONCLUSIONS
In summary, a series of $\mathrm{Co}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Fe}_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{P}$ were successfully synthesized via a sample phosphidation process by using their corresponding MOFs precursors. Benefiting from the large specific area, well-defined as well as porous structure of the MOFs precursors and the synergistic effect between Co and Fe elements, the obtained $\mathrm{Co}_{0.66}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.33}\mathrm{P}$ catalyst displays excellent OER performances in alkaline solution. It shows a low onset potential, a small Tafel slope and super durability. To reach a current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , the $\mathrm{Co}_{0.66}^{\pm}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.33}\mathrm{P}$ catalyst only requires a small overpotential of $294~\mathrm{mV}$ . These results demonstrate the as-prepared bimetal phosphide $\mathrm{Co}_{0.66}\mathrm{Fe}0._{\cdot33}\mathrm{P}$ is suitable for OER and may be applied to practice.
